LONG = {}
LONG["andrew"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Andrew was", """Andrew was Peter’s brother, and like Peter he was a fisherman from Bethsaida on the north shore of the Sea of Galilee who later lived in Capernaum. His name is Greek, meaning “manly” or “brave” — a sign that Galilee was a crossroads where Jewish families often used Greek names.

Andrew was a seeker. Before he ever met Jesus, he had made the long trip south to the Jordan River to follow John the Baptist, who was calling Israel to repent because the Messiah was coming. That tells us Andrew was spiritually hungry and paying attention, waiting for God to act."""),
("How Andrew met Jesus", """Andrew was standing with John the Baptist when Jesus walked by, and John said, “Look, the Lamb of God!” Andrew and another disciple (probably John, son of Zebedee) immediately started following Jesus. Jesus turned around and asked them the first question He asks in John’s Gospel: “What are you looking for?” They asked where He was staying, and He said, “Come and see.” They spent the rest of the day with Him — John even remembers it was about four in the afternoon.

That makes Andrew one of the first two people ever to follow Jesus. And his very first act as a follower was to go and find his brother and bring him to Jesus.""", ("John",1)),
("Why Jesus chose Andrew", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose Andrew, but it shows us what kind of man he was. Andrew had already left home to follow a prophet because he wanted God. He recognized Jesus quickly and acted on it. And every time he appears in John’s Gospel, he is bringing someone else to Jesus: his brother Peter, a boy with a small lunch, and a group of Greek visitors. Jesus called His first disciples to become “fishers of people” — and Andrew was doing it before he was even asked.

Andrew was officially called by the lake, together with Peter, when Jesus walked by their boat and said, “Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of people.” At once they left their nets. He was part of Jesus’ circle of four (Peter, Andrew, James and John) and was with them on the Mount of Olives when Jesus spoke about the end of the age."""),
], outro=[("Why Andrew matters today", """Andrew lived in his famous brother’s shadow. Peter preached to thousands; Andrew is barely quoted. But Peter was there because Andrew brought him. Andrew shows that one of the most important things anyone can do is quietly bring another person to Jesus — and then step back and let Jesus do the rest.""")])

LONG["james-zebedee"] = dict(intro=[
("Who James was", """James was a fisherman on the Sea of Galilee, the son of Zebedee and the older brother of John. Zebedee ran a fishing business large enough to employ hired workers, so the family was reasonably well-off. Their mother, likely named Salome, later followed Jesus too, supporting Him and standing near the cross when He died. Some scholars think she was a sister of Mary, which would make James and John cousins of Jesus — but the Bible doesn’t say that directly.

He is often called “James the Greater” to tell him apart from the other apostle named James, the son of Alphaeus."""),
("How James met Jesus", """James and John were partners with Peter and Andrew. After the miraculous catch of fish, when Peter’s boat nearly sank, James and John were there too, amazed. Jesus walked along the shore and saw the two brothers in a boat with their father, preparing their nets. He called them, and immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Him. Mark adds the small, human detail that they left Zebedee “in the boat with the hired men.”""", ("Mark",1)),
("Why Jesus chose James", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose James, but it shows he was wholehearted and fierce. Jesus nicknamed him and John “Boanerges,” meaning “Sons of Thunder” — most likely because of their fiery tempers and intense loyalty. Jesus didn’t choose them because they were gentle; He chose them and then taught them what real greatness looks like. James became one of the three disciples closest to Jesus."""),
], outro=[("Why James matters today", """James asked for a throne and was given a cup of suffering instead — and he drank it faithfully, as the first of the twelve to die for Jesus. His story reminds us that following Jesus is not about status but about faithfulness, even to the end.""")])

LONG["john"] = dict(intro=[
("Who John was", """John was the younger brother of James and the son of Zebedee, a fisherman with a family business on the Sea of Galilee. He was probably the youngest of the twelve apostles, perhaps still a teenager or in his early twenties when he started following Jesus.

He is one of the most important people in the New Testament. John wrote (or is traditionally credited with) five books: the Gospel of John, the letters 1, 2 and 3 John, and the Book of Revelation. Interestingly, in his own Gospel he never once uses his own name. He calls himself “the disciple whom Jesus loved.”"""),
("How John met Jesus", """John’s Gospel tells of two disciples of John the Baptist who first followed Jesus. One was Andrew; the other is unnamed, and many believe it was John himself, writing modestly. Later, Jesus called John and his brother James from their father’s boat, and they left immediately to follow Him.""", ("John",1)),
("Why Jesus chose John", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose John, but it shows a young man with intense passion. Jesus nicknamed him and James “Sons of Thunder.” John once tried to stop a stranger from driving out demons in Jesus’ name because he “wasn’t one of us,” and he wanted to call down fire on a Samaritan village. Jesus took that fierce loyalty and, over a lifetime, turned it into love. The man who wanted to burn a village became the apostle who wrote “God is love.” John was part of the inner three, present at the raising of Jairus’s daughter, the Transfiguration, and in Gethsemane."""),
], outro=[("What made John extraordinary", """John was the only one of the twelve who stood at the cross, the one Jesus trusted with His own mother, the first to believe at the empty tomb, the first to recognize the risen Jesus by the lake, and the one who received the visions of Revelation. He outlived all the other apostles.""" ),
("Why John matters today", """John’s life shows how Jesus can transform a person over time. He began as a “Son of Thunder” and ended as the apostle of love. His Gospel was written, he says, “so that you may believe that Jesus is the Messiah, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in His name.”""", ("John",20))])

LONG["philip"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Philip was", """Philip came from Bethsaida, the same fishing town as Peter and Andrew. His name is Greek (“lover of horses”), which may be why later some Greek visitors in Jerusalem came to him first when they wanted to see Jesus. Philip appears only a few times in the Gospels, almost all in John’s Gospel, and he comes across as practical, analytical and honest."""),
("How Philip met Jesus", """The day after Andrew and Peter met Jesus, Jesus decided to go to Galilee. He found Philip and said simply, “Follow Me.” Philip is one of the few people whom Jesus actively sought out rather than being brought by someone else. Philip immediately went and found his friend Nathanael and told him, “We have found the one Moses wrote about in the Law, and about whom the prophets also wrote — Jesus of Nazareth.”""", ("John",1)),
("Why Jesus chose Philip", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose Philip. What we see is a man who believed quickly and invited others without arguing — “Come and see.” We also see Jesus patiently stretching him: testing him before feeding the 5,000, and gently answering his request at the Last Supper to “show us the Father.” Philip shows that Jesus chooses careful, practical thinkers too, and grows their faith beyond their calculations."""),
], outro=[("Why Philip matters today", """Philip’s “Come and see” is still one of the best ways to share faith: not winning an argument, but inviting someone to meet Jesus for themselves.""")])

LONG["bartholomew"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Bartholomew was", """Bartholomew appears in every list of the twelve apostles, but the first three Gospels tell us nothing else about him. His name is actually a family name: “Bar-Tolmai,” son of Tolmai. John’s Gospel never mentions Bartholomew, but tells a detailed story about a man named Nathanael from Cana in Galilee, a friend of Philip. Because Bartholomew is always listed right beside Philip, most scholars believe Nathanael Bar-Tolmai is one person."""),
("How he met Jesus", """His friend Philip came to him excited: “We have found the Messiah — Jesus of Nazareth.” Nathanael was skeptical: “Nazareth! Can anything good come from there?” Philip said, “Come and see.” As Nathanael approached, Jesus said, “Here truly is an Israelite in whom there is no deceit.” Surprised, Nathanael asked how He knew him. Jesus answered, “I saw you while you were still under the fig tree before Philip called you.” Something about that detail — perhaps a private moment of prayer — overwhelmed him, and he declared, “Rabbi, You are the Son of God; You are the King of Israel.”""", ("John",1)),
("Why Jesus chose him", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why, but Jesus’ first words describe him: a man with nothing false about him. Nathanael said exactly what he thought, even when it was rude. Jesus valued that honesty, met his skepticism with a personal sign, and promised him he would see far greater things — heaven opened and angels ascending and descending on the Son of Man."""),
], outro=[("Why he matters today", """Bartholomew shows that honest doubt isn’t a barrier to Jesus. Jesus knew him before they met and welcomed his questions.""")])

LONG["thomas"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Thomas was", """Thomas was one of the twelve apostles, also called Didymus, which is Greek for “twin.” (Thomas comes from the Aramaic word for twin too.) The Bible never tells us who his twin was, where he came from, or what work he did before following Jesus, though he was among the disciples who went fishing in Galilee after the resurrection, so he may have been a fisherman."""),
("How Thomas met Jesus", """The Gospels don’t record Thomas’s calling. He simply appears in the list of twelve whom Jesus chose after a night of prayer. Everything we know about his personality comes from four short scenes in John’s Gospel — and in each one, Thomas says exactly what he thinks.""", ("Luke",6)),
("Why Jesus chose Thomas", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why Jesus chose Thomas, but his story shows a loyal, brave, blunt man who wanted real answers. He was willing to die with Jesus, he asked the honest questions others were afraid to ask, and when he finally saw the risen Jesus, he made the clearest declaration of Jesus’ divinity in the Gospels. Jesus didn’t reject Thomas for doubting — He came back specifically for him."""),
], outro=[("Why Thomas matters today", """He’s remembered as “Doubting Thomas,” but his story ends with faith, not doubt. Jesus meets honest questions with evidence and invitation: “Touch and see.” And Jesus added a blessing for everyone who came after: “Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed.”""", ("John",20))])

LONG["matthew"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Matthew was", """Matthew, also called Levi son of Alphaeus, was a tax collector in Capernaum, the lakeside town where Peter lived. He worked for Herod Antipas, who ruled Galilee under Rome. His booth was likely on the busy trade road along the lake, collecting tolls and customs on goods and fish.

Tax collectors were among the most despised people in Jewish society. They worked for foreign rulers and usually got rich by charging more than required and keeping the difference. Religious Jews considered them traitors and lumped them together with prostitutes and “sinners.” Because Matthew handled money and records, he would have been literate and organized — skills that later suited a Gospel writer."""),
("How Matthew met Jesus", """Jesus had been teaching and healing all around Capernaum, so Matthew surely knew about Him. One day Jesus walked past the tax booth, looked at Matthew, and said, “Follow Me.” Matthew got up, left everything, and followed Him. For a fisherman, quitting was temporary — the boats would still be there. For Matthew, walking away from the tax booth meant losing his job and income for good.""", ("Matthew",9)),
("Why Jesus chose Matthew", """The Bible doesn’t say exactly why, but Jesus explained His reason for spending time with people like Matthew: “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners.” By choosing a hated tax collector to be one of His closest friends, Jesus showed that no one was beyond His reach. And He placed Matthew on the same team as Simon the Zealot, who hated Rome — a picture of how Jesus brings enemies together."""),
], outro=[("Why Matthew matters today", """Matthew’s Gospel ends with Jesus’ command to “go and make disciples of all nations” and the promise, “I am with you always.” The man who once collected money from people spent the rest of his life giving them something far more valuable.""", ("Matthew",28))])

LONG["james-alphaeus"] = dict(intro=[("Who he was", """James son of Alphaeus is one of the least-known apostles. He is listed in all four lists of the twelve but never quoted or described. He is often identified with “James the younger” (or “James the Less”), whose mother Mary stood watching at the crucifixion. Since Matthew (Levi) is also called “son of Alphaeus,” some have wondered whether they were brothers, but the Bible never says so.

The Bible doesn’t say how he met Jesus or why Jesus chose him. What we know is that he was there through everything — and that Jesus chose him by name, after praying all night.""")],
 outro=[("Why he matters today", """Most of Jesus’ followers throughout history have been like James son of Alphaeus: faithful, present, and unknown. His place among the twelve is a reminder that being chosen and loved by Jesus doesn’t depend on being famous.""")])

LONG["jude-thaddaeus"] = dict(intro=[("Who he was", """This apostle goes by different names in different Gospels. Matthew and Mark call him Thaddaeus (in some manuscripts, Lebbaeus); Luke and Acts call him Judas son of James. John refers to him as “Judas (not Judas Iscariot)” to avoid confusion with the betrayer. Most English speakers know him as Jude. He should not be confused with Jude, the brother of Jesus, who wrote the letter of Jude, though some traditions have combined the two.

The Bible doesn’t say how he met Jesus or why Jesus chose him. He speaks only once in the Gospels — and it’s a good question.""")],
 outro=[("Why he matters today", """Jude’s question — why Jesus reveals Himself to some and not to the whole world — is one many people still ask. Jesus’ answer is that He makes His home with anyone who loves Him and keeps His word.""")])

LONG["simon-zealot"] = dict(intro=[("Who he was", """Simon is always identified as “the Zealot” (Luke) or “the Cananean” (Matthew and Mark, from an Aramaic word meaning “zealous”). The title separates him from Simon Peter. In first-century Judea, “zealots” were Jews passionate for God’s law and Israel’s freedom; some favored violent resistance to Roman occupation. A few decades after Jesus, the Zealot movement led the Jewish revolt that ended with Rome destroying Jerusalem in AD 70.

The Bible doesn’t say how Simon met Jesus or why Jesus chose him. But the label tells us he came to Jesus with strong political convictions — and ended up sharing meals every day with Matthew, a man who had worked for the very system he hated.""")],
 outro=[("Why he matters today", """Jesus’ kingdom didn’t come the way Simon may have expected — not with swords, but with a cross. Simon’s presence among the twelve shows that following Jesus can unite people across the deepest political divides.""")])

LONG["judas"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Judas was", """Judas Iscariot was one of the twelve apostles. “Iscariot” most likely means “man of Kerioth,” a town in southern Judea, which would make him the only one of the twelve not from Galilee. His father was named Simon. Every list of the apostles places him last and adds a note: “who betrayed Him.”

Judas was not an outsider who slipped in. He was chosen by Jesus after a night of prayer, sent out with the others to preach and heal, and trusted to keep the group’s money bag."""),
("Why Jesus chose Judas", """This is one of the hardest questions in the Bible, and the Bible doesn’t say exactly why. John’s Gospel says Jesus knew from the beginning who would betray Him, and Jesus once said, “Have I not chosen you, the Twelve? Yet one of you is a devil.” Jesus fulfilled the Scriptures, including a psalm about a close friend who shared bread and then turned against Him. Yet Jesus treated Judas as a friend to the very end: He washed his feet, shared bread with him at the Last Supper, and even greeted him in the garden by calling him “friend.” Judas was given every opportunity.""", ("John",6)),
], outro=[("Why Judas matters today", """Judas is a warning that it’s possible to be very close to Jesus — to see the miracles and hear the teaching — without truly following Him. But his story also sits beside Peter’s. Both failed Jesus on the same night. The difference was what they did next: Peter came back to Jesus and was restored; Judas, filled with remorse, turned away in despair. No failure is beyond Jesus’ forgiveness for anyone who returns to Him.""")])

LONG["matthias"] = dict(intro=[("Who Matthias was", """Matthias was not one of the original twelve, but he was a long-time follower of Jesus. Peter said the person replacing Judas needed to have been with them the whole time — from John’s baptism until Jesus was taken up to heaven — and to be a witness of the resurrection. That means Matthias had quietly followed Jesus for about three years without being named an apostle. Some have suggested he was one of the 72 disciples Jesus sent out to preach, though the Bible doesn’t say. After his selection, the Bible never mentions him again.""")],
 outro=[("Why he matters today", """Matthias was faithful long before he had a title. When the moment came, he was ready. His story honors everyone who follows Jesus faithfully in the background.""")])
